/**
@page supported_families Supported families

@tableofcontents

The first release of libopenwch targets two WCH families.  They share the
QingKe core layer and the reusable build system, but have intentionally
separate archives because their ISA and memory maps differ.

@section families_summary Family summary

| Family | Parts | Core / ISA | Archive | API reference |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| CH32V00x | CH32V002, CH32V003, CH32V004, CH32V005, CH32V006, CH32V007 | QingKe V2, RV32EC | `libopenwch_ch32v0.a` | <a href="../ch32v0/html/index.html">open</a> |
| CH58x | CH582, CH583, CH584, CH585 | QingKe V4, RV32IMAC | `libopenwch_ch5xx58x.a` | <a href="../ch5xx58x/html/index.html">open</a> |

The build system selects the archive, ISA flags, and linker script from
<code>ld/devices.data</code> using the application's <code>DEVICE</code>
variable.  Applications must not supply their own <code>-march</code> or
<code>-mabi</code>.

@section families_ch32v00x CH32V00x

* QingKe V2 core in RV32EC mode: 16 registers, compressed instructions, no
  hardware divide.
* The CH32V003 is built as plain <code>rv32ec_zicsr_zifencei</code>.  Parts
  that advertise <code>zmmul</code> get it from their device entry, but the
  shared archive never claims it.
* GPIO ports A, C, and D are eight bits wide.  Each pin's CNF/MODE nibble is
  handled as an opaque value; see @ref project_overview.
* There is no <code>.highcode</code> section requirement: the flash controller
  is not reconfigured after boot.
* Public API: 316 functions in the current tree.
* Reference: <a href="../ch32v0/html/index.html">CH32V00x API documentation</a>.

@section families_ch58x CH58x

* QingKe V4 core in RV32IMAC mode.
* A 32 MHz HSE crystal is mandatory; the PLL runs at a fixed 480 MHz.  Bring-up
  must power the crystal before selecting the PLL as the system clock.
* Most system, clock, power, and GPIO registers are RWA (write-protected).
  Every access goes through the safe-access helpers in
  @ref api_index ("RWA") rather than writing the register directly.
* Code that reconfigures flash or enters low-power states must run from RAM; it
  is tagged <code>OPENWCH_HIGH_CODE</code> and placed in the linker's
  <code>.highcode</code> section.
* BLE is available as a thin wrapper over WCH's closed-source
  <code>LIBCH58xBLE.a</code>.  The binary keeps its Apache-2.0 licence and is
  linked separately, opt-in through <code>LIBOPENWCH_BLE=1</code> in the
  application template.  See @ref project_architecture and the
  <a href="../ch5xx58x/html/group__ble__file.html">CH58x BLE API</a>.
* Public API: 230 functions in the current tree (including 28 BLE-layer
  functions).
* Reference: <a href="../ch5xx58x/html/index.html">CH58x API documentation</a>.

@section families_future Future families

The device database and directory structure already anticipate additional WCH
families such as CH32X035, CH32L103, CH32V103/203/208/303/305/307, CH571/573,
and CH591/592.  Adding a family is a mechanical checklist: see
@ref contributing and <code>HACKING_COMMON_DOC</code>.
*/
